in late October workers in China lined  up for quarantine then others rushed to  leave their jobs and some clashed with  police and covet protective suits  these are images of the chaos that  erupted at a Foxconn site in Zhengzhou  China where the majority of Apple's  iPhones are made  now holiday Shoppers this Christmas  season are stuck with long wait times  and apple warned its pro model shipments  would be lower than expected  so here's what led to the protests at  Foxconn and what that means for your  iPhone  foreign  the travel season for China's week-long  national day holiday began and people  hit the road  when residents return from other parts  of the country that's when covid-19  broke out in zhangzhou the virus  eventually spread to foxconn's fast  manufacturing site where the company  enforced strict coveted controls  by late October some workers were sent  to quarantine centers others were  confined to unfinished apartment  buildings nearby and they were filmed  screaming in frustration  meanwhile some employees were asked to  move out of their dorms and sleep in the  factory hallways to keep production  running  worried about catching covet on October  29th thousands of workers fled the  facility  some people boarded buses after hearing  rumors that infected employees were  being allowed to work to meet production  goals  to replace those who left recruitment  ads started popping up on November 7th  these ads promise quarantine allowances  and bonuses for people who would work  for 30 or 60 days  foreign  on November 23rd when new recruits got  angry over what they said were delayed  bonus payments and conditions at the  plant  [Applause]  to control the outrage police were  deployed beating and chasing workers  the next day many employees lined up at  a train station after taking a foxconn's  offer of fourteen hundred dollars to  leave the company also said it would  guarantee recruits would be paid in line  with official ads and blamed a computer  error for some of the pay issues  the staff shortage has added pressure to  the tech Giant's iPhone production as  Foxconn jeongzhou facility makes 85  percent of the pro lineup of iPhones  this means customers who ordered their  smartphones in October or November may  not even get it before Christmas  to see why take a look at this graph  when Apple launched the iPhone 14 pro in  September the wait time was 32 days  during the first week then it started to  decrease this is pretty typical when  there's a new launch like with the  iPhone 13 Pro but when covet hit the  jeongzhou plant in October the wait time  went up and eventually hit 37 Days by  late November which is the longest in  the product's 15-year history Apple  didn't respond to a request for comment  about the iPhone delays  meanwhile steps are being taken to  mitigate the Ripple effects of the  disruptions Foxconn has tried to  increase iPhone 14 production at other  sites in the country according to people  familiar with the matter China's rigid  covet controls among other issues have  also prompted Apple to look beyond the  mainland particularly in India and  Vietnam and this could mean in the  future many of our iPhones start coming  not from China but from other countries
 

